               <history>
                  <provenance type="#i"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_110">Campsey, Suffolk. Priory
                        of St Mary the Virgin, of Augustinian nuns</orgName><p>The 1744 sale
                        catalogue of the Harley library describes an inscription that was
                        subsequently washed off: 'On the last Leaf, there is a Memorandum, written
                        by a Nun of Campesey, named <persName role="fmo">Elyzabeth
                           Wyllowby</persName>, That she gives this Book to Dame <persName
                           role="fmo">Cateryne Symonde</persName>, under the Condition, That, in no
                        wise, she sell it, or give it from the House of Campessey: But she shall
                        give it to one of her Sisters.' (<title>Catalogus Bibliothecae
                           Harleianae</title>, III, no. 1560.) Under ultra-violet light, 'Elizabeth
                        Willowby' and 'dame Cateryne Symonde' are more or less legible (sig. H6v of
                           <title>Treatise of Love</title>). The writing matches that in Cambridge,
                        Corpus Christi College, MS 268, also once owned by Elizabeth Willowby. We
                        are grateful to John McQuillen for bringing the identification to our
                        attention (2022). See especially McQuillen <ref
                           target="https://www.themorgan.org/blog/missing-nuns-reappear">'Missing
                           Nuns Reappear'</ref>, The Morgan Library &amp; Museum blog, 23 September
                        2021. This Campsey book was previously untraced; see especially the second
                        edition of Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1964).</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo">Thomas
                        Rawlinson</persName> (1681–1725), his sale, part VIII, 26 April 1727, lot
                     2072.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Acquired by <persName role="fmo">Edward
                        Harley</persName> (1689–1741), 2nd earl of Oxford. With other printed books
                     from his library, sold by his widow, Henrietta Cavendish (1694–1755), in 1743
                     to Thomas Osborne.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo">George Spencer
                        Churchill</persName> (1766-1840), 5th duke of Marlborough and Marquess of
                     Blandford.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">White Knights Library sale, Evans, part I, 7
                     June 1819, lot 970 for £32/11/0 to:</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo">Heneage Finch</persName>
                     (1786–1859), 5th earl of Aylesford, for whom the book was rebound (his crest is
                     on the binding), which is probably when the inscription was removed. </provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Heneage Finch (1849-1885), 7th earl of
                     Aylesford, his sale, Christie's, 12 March 1888, lot 409, to Quaritch. </provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo">William Amhurst
                        Tyssen-Amherst</persName>, Baron Amherst (1835–1909), his sale, Sotheby's
                     London, 3 Dec. 1908, lot 180 (purchased prior to sale) to: Pierpont Morgan
                     (1837–1913).</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Purchased for Princeton with the Amherst
                     collection, 1908.</provenance>
               </history>
